[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The AI takeover is inevitable… or at&nbsp; least that’s what you’ve been told.&nbsp; Once you upload a photo or voice note,&nbsp; it’s gone forever, scraped into AI systems&nbsp;&nbsp; feeding an insatiable hunger for data. But what if that’s not the full story?&nbsp; Right now, a digital insurgency is taking place.&nbsp; Traps are being laid inside the data these models&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depend on. Researchers have figured out how&nbsp; to“lobotomize” AI models using just a handful&nbsp;&nbsp; of modified JPEGs. And it works.&nbsp; So what happens when the scrapers&nbsp; don’t just learn from the internet…&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but start breaking because of it? Let’s say someone types a simple request into&nbsp;&nbsp; an AI model. They ask for a photorealistic dog,&nbsp; running through a park. It’s the kind of thing&nbsp;&nbsp; a competent model nails every single time. But this time, the results are different.&nbsp;'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The legs don’t connect properly. Extra joints&nbsp; appear where they shouldn’t exist. The face subtly&nbsp;&nbsp; drifts into something that’s from the Uncanny&nbsp; Valley. The fur loses structure and definition.&nbsp; It looks like something that’s&nbsp; seen a dog a thousand times,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but doesn’t actually understand what it is. That’s the work of a poisoned AI model.&nbsp; It’s not a bug. The model is still&nbsp; doing what it was trained to do.&nbsp; The problem is what it was trained on A poisoned AI model is what happens when&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'someone messes with the dataset it learns from.&nbsp; They slip in manipulated or misleading examples&nbsp;&nbsp; during training so the model starts picking up&nbsp; the wrong patterns. No one has to hack anything or&nbsp;&nbsp; break into a system. Artists and creators simply&nbsp; post their work online like they always have.&nbsp;'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then the scrapers arrive. These are automated bots that crawl the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp; scooping up massive amounts of images, text,&nbsp; and audio from websites. They don’t understand&nbsp;&nbsp; what they’re gathering, they just vacuum it&nbsp; up to build training datasets for AI models.&nbsp;'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'That’s where the problem starts. Mixed in with all that normal content,&nbsp;&nbsp; the poisoned examples get collected too. Nothing&nbsp; looks wrong at the time. The trap only reveals&nbsp;&nbsp; itself later, during the next training run. A team at the University of Chicago put&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the theory to the test. They fed Stable&nbsp; Diffusion about 50 altered pictures of dogs.&nbsp;&nbsp; Then they put the AI model to work. Almost&nbsp; immediately, the images were warped. Every&nbsp;&nbsp; single one had something wrong with it. The team kept going. Once they got to&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around 300 poisoned images the model&nbsp; started producing images of a cat.&nbsp; A model trains on billions of images, but for any&nbsp; single concept, say, a dog, it only really relies&nbsp;&nbsp; on a few thousand of them. Corrupt a small slice&nbsp; and it all starts to fall apart. You don’t need&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'millions of bad files. You need less than 1%&nbsp; of the right ones, aimed at the right concept.&nbsp; These models aren’t isolated. They’re powering&nbsp; Midjourney, DALL·E, and the image tools built&nbsp;&nbsp; into your phone. So if one system becomes&nbsp; corrupted, the effects don’t stay contained.&nbsp;'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'They spread. It’s the opening shot of a new kind of fight.&nbsp;&nbsp; And the people doing it aren’t rival labs or&nbsp; bad actors. They’re illustrators, photographers,&nbsp;&nbsp; creatives and ordinary users with a free app…&nbsp; and they all have a reason to be furious.'

[02:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But before we go any further, imagine this.&nbsp; You’re online every single day. You check&nbsp; your email, open a few apps, look things up,&nbsp;&nbsp; stream videos, maybe even do all of that while&nbsp; traveling. And to you, it feels totally normal.&nbsp; But behind the scenes, your internet provider,&nbsp; advertisers, network admins, and sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'even governments can build a surprisingly&nbsp; detailed picture of what you’re doing online.&nbsp; Now, to be clear, no VPN can protect you from&nbsp; everything. You still have to be smart online.&nbsp;&nbsp; Don’t click suspicious links, don’t hand&nbsp; over personal information to sketchy emails,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and definitely don’t trust the&nbsp; so-called Prince of Nigeria.&nbsp; But a good VPN is an important layer of privacy,&nbsp; because privacy shouldn’t be something you only&nbsp;&nbsp; think about after something goes&nbsp; wrong. It should be the default.&nbsp; And that’s exactly what Proton VPN is built for. Proton VPN helps keep your browsing private&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wherever you are, whether you’re at home,&nbsp; traveling, or just trying to stop your&nbsp;&nbsp; online activity from being tracked. Their no-logs&nbsp; policy has been verified by independent auditors,&nbsp;&nbsp; and Proton is backed by a foundation dedicated&nbsp; to privacy, transparency, and user rights.'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Proton VPN is also fully open source, meaning&nbsp; its code is publicly available for inspection. So&nbsp;&nbsp; instead of just asking you to trust them, Proton&nbsp; gives people a way to verify what they’re doing.&nbsp; And privacy doesn’t have to mean slow.&nbsp; Proton VPN offers high-speed connections&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with VPN Accelerator, plus NetShield,&nbsp; which blocks malicious ads and trackers&nbsp;&nbsp; before they ruin your browsing experience. It also gives you more freedom online. You can&nbsp;&nbsp; connect through servers around the world, access&nbsp; content more securely while traveling, and use it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for streaming, gaming, and P2P file sharing, all&nbsp; while keeping your online activity more private.&nbsp; So whether you’re trying to stop advertisers&nbsp; from following you, keep your browsing private&nbsp;&nbsp; from your ISP, or just take back control of your&nbsp; online life, Proton VPN makes privacy simple.&nbsp;'

[04:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Click the link in the description or&nbsp; go to protonvpn.com/theinfographicsshow&nbsp;&nbsp; to get up to 70% discount when you&nbsp; sign up to Proton VPN 2-year plan.&nbsp; Nobody poisons their own work for fun.&nbsp; They do it because they felt like their&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[04:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work was taken from them. That feeling doesn’t come&nbsp;&nbsp; from nowhere. It all started with a theft. For years, the firms building these models&nbsp;&nbsp; treated the open web as a warehouse.&nbsp; They scraped billions of images, voices,&nbsp;&nbsp; and paragraphs from it. The original creators&nbsp; were never asked, and they were never paid.&nbsp;'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It was a heist, plain and simple. Much of that haul went into one open dataset&nbsp;&nbsp; called LAION-5B, which held a massive dataset,&nbsp; made of up to 6 billion images and accompanying&nbsp;&nbsp; text. Stable Diffusion learned from it, and so&nbsp; did most of the art tools that followed. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a compressed version of the visible internet,&nbsp; folded into something machines learn from.&nbsp; Then the artists paying attention. They started recognizing their own work in the&nbsp;&nbsp; output. Styles and techniques that took decades to&nbsp; develop were being reproduced in seconds. By 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a generator could mimic the brushstrokes and&nbsp; talent of an artist in a heartbeat. A career’s&nbsp;&nbsp; worth of experience was reduced to a prompt and&nbsp; undercut by a tool that cost nothing to use.&nbsp; Conceptual artist Karla Ortiz, along with&nbsp; other artists, sued Stability AI in 2023.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Getty Images filed its own case around the same&nbsp; time. The lawsuits dragged out, and all the while,&nbsp;&nbsp; the scraping continued and the models improved. The companies behind the models freely admitted&nbsp;&nbsp; to their practices. OpenAI told the British&nbsp; parliament that building today\'s top models&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'without copyrighted work would be impossible.&nbsp; Sure, there were opt-out forms but it put the&nbsp;&nbsp; onus on the creator. Instead of asking permission&nbsp; upfront, artists had to hunt down models, file&nbsp;&nbsp; requests one by one, and hope they were honored.&nbsp; The balance of power remained with the developers.&nbsp;'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So the artists adapted. Ordinary uploads turned&nbsp;&nbsp; into weapons, one file at a time. In 2023, the University of Chicago&nbsp;&nbsp; released a program called Glaze and it&nbsp; was aimed directly at the AI models.&nbsp;'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Type an artist’s name into an AI model and&nbsp; it’ll generate a painting based on that&nbsp;&nbsp; particular style and the data it’s learned on.&nbsp; Glaze changes that. It works by exploiting how&nbsp;&nbsp; a computer sees an image. To an AI, a painting&nbsp; isn’t really a painting at all. It’s a series&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of numbers that make up the style and structure.&nbsp; Glaze distorts the numbers slightly. It’s subtle,&nbsp;&nbsp; something that isn’t visible to the human eye, but&nbsp; the machine learns from the cloaked information.&nbsp; It works like an optical illusion. Two&nbsp; viewers can look at the same image and&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[06:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'see completely different things. In this&nbsp; case, the viewers are you and the machine.&nbsp;&nbsp; You see your painting exactly as it was intended. The model sees something else.&nbsp; An oil painting might seem like it’s charcoal.&nbsp; A watercolor might be seen as a completely&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different medium. The visual style the scraper&nbsp; came to learn from has effectively been moved.&nbsp; Word spread fast, and Glaze has now passed&nbsp; 6 million downloads. But there are limits.&nbsp;&nbsp; Glaze could hide a style, but it couldn’t stop&nbsp; someone from taking the image itself. And every&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time people found a new way to alter the image,&nbsp; scrapers came back with better ways to recover it.&nbsp;&nbsp; It was like playing defense forever. The creators need something&nbsp;&nbsp; that could end the fight. They needed a poison pill.&nbsp;'

[07:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'It came in January 2024, and was Nightshade. Another product of the University of Chicago,&nbsp;&nbsp; it had one goal. To infect. An image run through Nightshade&nbsp;&nbsp; will look completely normal, to both human eyes&nbsp; and a machine. But it’s like a trojan horse. If&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a machine is trained on that one image,&nbsp; and the model starts learning nonsense.&nbsp; The results are almost comical. Hats&nbsp; turn into cakes, handbags into toasters,&nbsp;&nbsp; and a car learns to draw itself as a cow. For artists and creators, that felt like payback.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[07:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For years, AI companies had scraped artwork to&nbsp; train their models. Now the very thing being taken&nbsp;&nbsp; could be turned against the people taking it. The demand was instant.&nbsp; Nightshade hit 250,000 downloads in just 5 days.&nbsp; University servers buckled under the traffic.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The team behind it had to scramble to post backup&nbsp; download links as thousands more rushed to get it.&nbsp; Most people assume this is a form of hacking.&nbsp; It’s not. No firewall gets breached, and the&nbsp;&nbsp; tool never touches a single company server.&nbsp; Researchers call it adversarial machine learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but a simpler way to look at it is that&nbsp; Nightshade is a magic trick for machines.&nbsp; The model isn\'t attacked from the outside. It\'s&nbsp; fooled into teaching itself the wrong thing, and&nbsp;&nbsp; then it trusts that mistake as if it were true. It doesn\'t take much to start the process.&nbsp;'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Fewer than 100 carefully crafted images can&nbsp; be enough to poison a single concept inside&nbsp;&nbsp; a top AI model. And if you pair Nightshade with&nbsp; Glaze, the same image can hide an artist\'s style&nbsp;&nbsp; and poison the training data at the same time. But those results came from models researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'could test directly. The bigger&nbsp; AI systems are harder to study,&nbsp;&nbsp; and nobody outside those companies knows exactly&nbsp; how vulnerable they are. But that\'s not really the&nbsp;&nbsp; point. Nightshade took the idea from a theory&nbsp; on paper to something that actually worked.&nbsp;'

[09:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The damage doesn\'t stay contained either. Poison&nbsp; the concept of a dog, and related ideas like&nbsp;&nbsp; huskies, puppies, and wolves can start drifting&nbsp; with it. In some tests, researchers fed hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp; of corrupted images in a single model until it&nbsp; could barely generate recognizable images at all.&nbsp;'

[09:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And that\'s where this stopped being purely&nbsp; defensive. Every poisoned image uploaded to&nbsp;&nbsp; the internet becomes a potential sleeper&nbsp; cell. It can remain unnoticed inside a&nbsp;&nbsp; dataset for months or years, waiting for the&nbsp; next training run. The person who uploaded it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[09:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'might never know if it was collected, or&nbsp; which model eventually learned from it.&nbsp; Images were only the start. The same trick&nbsp; works on anything a machine learns from,&nbsp;&nbsp; from your writing to your face. And your voice is the next target.&nbsp;'

[09:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A service like ElevenLabs needs only a few clean&nbsp; seconds of your voice to build a convincing copy.&nbsp;&nbsp; A podcast clip can be enough. So can a YouTube&nbsp; video, a few seconds of footage on social media or&nbsp;&nbsp; even an old voicemail buried in someone\'s phone. It’s becoming a favorite tool of scammers.&nbsp;'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And it isn’t a hypothetical risk. In 2024, criminals used cloned voices&nbsp;&nbsp; and faces to steal about $25 million in&nbsp; a single faked video call. Banks and law&nbsp;&nbsp; enforcement agencies now warn people about&nbsp; calls from cloned relatives asking for money.&nbsp;'

[10:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A tool called SafeSpeech was built by&nbsp; security researchers for exactly that reason.&nbsp;&nbsp; Your voice has its own signature, a kind&nbsp; of audio fingerprint that AI systems use&nbsp;&nbsp; to recognize and copy you. SafeSpeech subtly&nbsp; smudges that fingerprint. To another person,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you sound exactly the same. But to a voice-cloning&nbsp; model, you become much harder to copy.&nbsp; To the machine, it works a bit like radio&nbsp; interference. You hear the voice clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp; but the AI doesn\'t. The details it needs most get&nbsp; scrambled. Train a voice clone on that recording,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and the result comes out wrong. They’re close&nbsp; enough to sound human, but missing the subtle&nbsp;&nbsp; traits that make you… sound like you. The timing is what makes it clever.&nbsp; This isn\'t a filter slapped onto a fake after&nbsp; it\'s generated. It’s in the original recording&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[10:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'itself. The AI learns from the protected&nbsp; version, which means the cloning process&nbsp;&nbsp; breaks before it ever succeeds. There is&nbsp; no clean copy for the model to learn from.&nbsp; And that\'s what makes this different from&nbsp; protecting artwork. A stolen portfolio is&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'one kind of loss. Your voice is another. It\'s the&nbsp; sound your family and friends recognize instantly,&nbsp;&nbsp; the thing a scammer wants most when&nbsp; they\'re trying to impersonate you.&nbsp; It turns the tables. The target gets to set the trap.&nbsp;'

[11:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'For the first time, artists, writers, and everyday&nbsp; people had a way to fight back against systems&nbsp;&nbsp; trained on their work and their identities. It&nbsp; sounded like the next step in digital security.&nbsp; Then the AI labs responded. They weren\'t about to lose&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'access to the data that fueled their models.&nbsp; If artists could poison the training set,&nbsp;&nbsp; the labs would try to remove the poison. And that&nbsp; kicked off something bigger than a security tool.&nbsp; An arms race. They couldn’t just accept corrupted datasets,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[11:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and they couldn’t afford to throw most of them&nbsp; away either. So they tried a straightforward fix.&nbsp;&nbsp; They began checking each scraped image against its&nbsp; caption, then dropped anything that didn’t match.&nbsp; In theory, poisoned data should stand out. In reality, it only catches part of it.&nbsp;'

[12:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Tests on these systems show it flags&nbsp; maybe 40 to 60% of poisoned images.&nbsp;&nbsp; The rest slips through. And there’s a second&nbsp; problem. It also deletes plenty of clean data,&nbsp;&nbsp; leaving the model with either contaminated&nbsp; date or not enough to learn from.&nbsp;'

[12:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So they tried something else. They started scrubbing every image through a&nbsp;&nbsp; cleaning model before training, trying to wash out&nbsp; anything suspicious. It sounds clever, but it’s&nbsp;&nbsp; slow and expensive. And it still has limitations.&nbsp; Because the poisoning adapts. Artists design it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to survive that cleaning step, so it reappears on&nbsp; the other side like a stain bleeding back through.&nbsp; Not long after Glaze launched, one research group&nbsp; said it had already broken through the cloaking,&nbsp;&nbsp; so the Chicago team pushed out a tougher&nbsp; version. Whenever the program was breached,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[12:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they developed a new patch. It was a war of attrition.&nbsp; Even if a voice has protection built&nbsp; in, there are ways to strip parts of&nbsp;&nbsp; it out. Push it through enough processing&nbsp; and some of that protection starts to fade.&nbsp;&nbsp; In some cases, it gets noticeably weaker.&nbsp; Not gone, but damaged enough to matter.&nbsp;'

[13:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The poisoners are winning, for&nbsp; now, and the reason is lopsided.&nbsp; You have small teams constantly trying&nbsp; new tricks in the open. On the other,&nbsp;&nbsp; a handful of AI labs trying to patch holes as they&nbsp; appear. So every time a lab rolls out a fix, it&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gets tested immediately. And almost immediately,&nbsp; a new version shows up, adjusted just enough to&nbsp;&nbsp; get around it, usually within weeks. And this all comes with a price tag.&nbsp; It doesn’t land on the labs first. It lands on&nbsp; the people training the models with the data.&nbsp;'

[13:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The whole AI business model was built&nbsp; on the promise that data would be free,&nbsp;&nbsp; endless, and clean. The poison breaks&nbsp; the idea of clean. Once that goes,&nbsp;&nbsp; the other two stop feeling true as well. Because now nothing can be trusted at face value.&nbsp;'

[13:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'A poisoned file looks identical to a real one.&nbsp; There’s no label or warning. So every dataset&nbsp;&nbsp; turns into something that has to be checked&nbsp; manually or with tools that still miss a lot.&nbsp;&nbsp; Training a top model already costs hundreds of&nbsp; millions of dollars, and every extra cleanup&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[13:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'step adds to that bill. A single poisoned&nbsp; batch can set a project back by weeks.&nbsp; That’s the point. The goal was never&nbsp;&nbsp; to wreck one model for fun. It was to make&nbsp; stolen data more expensive than paid data.&nbsp;'

[14:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'There’s already a legal, cleaner method being&nbsp; used. Adobe trained its Firefly tool on Midjourney&nbsp;&nbsp; Images. Shutterstock cut similar deals. If you&nbsp; want clean, quality data, you need to pay for it.&nbsp; That is a serious problem for&nbsp; the industry. Investors poured&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[14:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tens of billions into AI on a single bet,&nbsp; that the data would stay cheap forever.&nbsp; That bet isn’t looking like a sure thing anymore. For a long time, the belief was that if you&nbsp;&nbsp; created something, you own it. The scrapers&nbsp; tore that deal up without asking anyone.&nbsp;'

[14:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The AI companies acted as if human work&nbsp; was free, endless, and owned by no one.&nbsp;&nbsp; That was the original mistake. Paintings were&nbsp; scraped. Photos were downloaded. Voices, faces,&nbsp;&nbsp; videos were collected in huge amounts. If it was online, it was fair game.&nbsp;'

[14:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Consent seemed optional. In every other industry, the opposite&nbsp;&nbsp; is standard. A photographer licenses an image&nbsp; before a brand uses it. Musicians clear samples&nbsp;&nbsp; before a track goes live. Even film studios&nbsp; pay for every frame of stock footage they use.&nbsp;'

[15:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The work of millions of people got&nbsp; treated as if it belonged to no one.&nbsp; This battle wasn’t created by the artists. It was&nbsp; the AI labs and their trainers.The moment work&nbsp;&nbsp; was taken without asking, the balance changed.&nbsp; Everything after that was just a reaction. You&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can’t build an empire on the idea that people do&nbsp; not count, then act shocked when those same people&nbsp;&nbsp; fight back and question how the system works. Because the problem isn’t sitting in a contract&nbsp;&nbsp; or a policy page. It’s built into the way the&nbsp; models learn in the first place. Every safeguard&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[15:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before this tried to politely control behavior&nbsp; through copyright notices or opt-out forms.&nbsp;&nbsp; But none of it really stopped the scraping This is different.&nbsp; For the first time, a single person can&nbsp; influence what the system learns next.&nbsp;'

[15:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And the system doesn’t get to ignore it. With people turning the tables on AI scrapers,&nbsp;&nbsp; digital theft is getting harder,&nbsp; especially when it comes to scams.&nbsp;&nbsp; But this isn’t the first time people have&nbsp; pushed back. Watch “Times Scammers Messed&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[16:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'With the Wrong People” to find out what happens&nbsp; when karma is dished out. Or click on this video.'
